# 011 · A release bundle

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** Install from images · **Depends on:** [010](010-published-images.goal)

## What

Each release attaches one compose file that pins the images by digest, and a short readme. The quick start uses it: download, `compose up`, no build. Building from source stays for contributors.

## Why

A tester should start Thermite in minutes, without a build.

## Done when

- [ ] A tester starts control and a CPU rig from the bundle on Linux and on macOS with no build.
- [x] The quick start uses the bundle.

## Decisions

None yet.
- 2026-10-26, owner, on the Linux and GPU checks: "we can have someone else test them later, surely people will try and if it breaks they'll file an issue." They are left to testers after the release.

## Open questions

None.

## Progress

- 2026-10-19: Goal written from the v0.1.0 release plan.
- 2026-10-20: The bundle is built. The release workflow (goal 010) runs `scripts/release-bundle.ts` after the push: it fills `containers/release/compose.template.yaml` and `containers/release/README.template.md` with the pushed images' digests (`name@sha256:…`, which Docker and Podman both pull) and attaches `compose.yaml` and `README.md` to a draft GitHub Release; a run by hand keeps them as an artifact. The template is the repository's `compose.yaml` with images in place of builds, the release's images as control's rig image defaults, and an `init` service. `tests/release/bundle.test.ts` fails when the template drifts from `compose.yaml`, and the script refuses a drifted template or a version that does not match `package.json`.
- 2026-10-20: The tokens without a checkout: `compose up` needs `.dev/secrets/` before it creates the containers, so the bundle's `init` service (`docker compose run --rm init`, then `up -d`) runs the new `thermite local init`. It makes the same `.dev/` as `npm run dev:init` (a unit test compares them), keeps every file, has no network, and, as root for another user's folder (Docker on Linux), gives what it makes to that folder's owner. podman-compose drops services outside the active profiles, so `init` has no profile; `up` runs it again harmlessly.
- 2026-10-20: Pages: the quick start (`tutorials/first-run.md`) and the install page (`how-to/install/one-machine.md`) use the bundle: download `compose.yaml`, `run --rm init`, `up -d`. The later tutorials and the guides build the command line from a source checkout beside the bundle, with the bundle's `.dev/` linked in. Building from source moved to the contributor guide (`docs/local-development.md`), with a section on releases. The README's quick start uses the bundle too.
- 2026-10-20: A local check on macOS with Podman: a bundle made by the script, with locally built images in place of the published ones, ran `init` (17 files made, then 9 kept), started control, the harness and the supervisor, and `local-1` reported to control. Left: the same with the published images, once the first tag is pushed, on Linux and on macOS.
- 2026-10-20: The bundle pins the RunPod rig image too (`rig-cuda`: `thermite-rig@sha256:…`) as the default of control's `THERMITE_RUNPOD_RIG_IMAGE`, so a tester's control launches the release's own rig image with no setting, and the readme lists it with its tag. `compose.yaml` now passes `THERMITE_RUNPOD_RIG_IMAGE` to control, empty by default, so the drift test's one rule for image defaults covers it and no new rule is needed. `tests/release/bundle.test.ts` checks the rendered default, the readme, a missing `rig-cuda` digest, and that the template drifts when it drops the line or gives it another default. `scripts/quick-start-acceptance.mjs` pins the rig image by the CPU harness's digest, as it does the CUDA harness: no step there launches a pod. Pages: Settings (the compose file), Install on one machine (`.env`), Push the images to your own registry, Run a rig on RunPod, the contributor guide.
- 2026-10-26 05:39 UTC: The published v0.1.0 bundle, followed as Your first run in a fresh folder on macOS: under Podman (05:30 to 05:34 UTC) and under Docker Desktop (05:35 to 05:39 UTC), 17 of 17 checks each, from the release's compose.yaml through init, up, the sign-in and the Overview's line to Judge smoke's `all 12 candidates were judged as expected` and the verdict counts. Left: Linux with Docker, which testers will report.

